Check the product of the nonzero dimensions of a GGUF tensor and write a tensor of 0 bytes
Fold-into: upstream/gguf

The defects. tests/fuzz/core/run.sh test replays the reproducer of each one.

- A tensor with a dimension of 0 skips the element-count guard, and ggml_nelements overflows.

  The element-count guard of the reader skips a tensor with a dimension of 0, because its element
  count is 0. But ggml_nelements multiplies ne[0] * ne[1] * ne[2] * ne[3] from the left, thus for
  the shape (2^32, 2^41, 0, 1) the product of the first two dimensions overflows int64_t
  (undefined behavior) before the 0. The byte-size check of the reader calls ggml_nelements, and
  UBSan reports the overflow on a file of a few hundred bytes. ggml_nrows and the strides multiply
  the dimensions in other orders.

  The change: The guard multiplies the dimensions that are not 0, and refuses the tensor when that
  product does not fit int64_t. The byte-size check uses the same product, thus it also bounds the
  strides of a tensor of 0 elements.

  The reproducer: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_gguf--gguf-zero-dim-overflow.

- The writer stops on a tensor of 0 bytes that the reader gave no data.

  When each tensor of a file has 0 bytes, the binary blob of the reader has 0 bytes, and the
  tensors of the ggml context get no data pointer. A write of that context (gguf-split, or a
  round trip) then stops on GGML_ASSERT(info.t.data), although there is nothing to copy.

  The change: The two writers copy no data for a tensor of 0 bytes.

  The reproducer: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_gguf--gguf-empty-tensor-write.

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 with the patches of patches/series above this one.

